The programs of this account
user-29c-sd-tcl-the-development-tools
The list shows the records of your program files (bp, and any file whose name ends in .bp or that has a .out file beside it): what each is (program, subroutine, function, class, or an include or screen record that is not compiled by itself), when it was compiled, how big the compiled program is, whether it is current or stale (the source was changed after it was compiled), and where it is cataloged: private (your account's cat file), local (a VOC entry) or global (the system's own catalog, gcat, which this page only shows). A private copy marked older is an earlier compile than the one in bp.out; C catalogs the current one.
B compiles the program (basic), C catalogs it privately (catalog), L locally (catalog local), X removes a local entry and deletes the compiled program it points at (decatalog), and D removes a private or a local entry (delete.catalog). Each is the verb you could type, run for you after a box that shows the exact command; Cancel is what Enter chooses. X and D, and a catalog that would also remove an entry of the same name from another catalog, ask for the name, typed. The page adds no.query to basic and catalog so that a question they would ask cannot be left waiting where you cannot see it; the box says what that means for the command.
The gates line shows whether your session has the bp-edit gate, asked of the system. SD Core Solo has no edit gates, so the line always says on and compiling, cataloging and removing from the catalog are not refused for want of it. X is not run on a local entry that leads outside your account: the page says which command to type. C and L look for an entry of the same name by name, as the verbs do, and ask for the name typed when it would remove or replace one that points at another program. Global cataloging is the administrator's own command (type ADMIN first, then catalog at the prompt) and is not offered here.
/ or F11 shows only the programs whose name or file holds some text; Esc clears it. A name with a capital, a dollar or another character the page does not give to a command is listed and described, and the page says which verb to type for it.
